<<<
wardress. FEMININE DESIGNATIONS.
===
**wardress.** ^^FEMININE DESIGNATIONS^^.
>>>

<<<
-WARD(S). Words ending with -ward(s) may most of them be used as adverbs, adjectives, or nouns. The -s is usually present in the adverb, & absent in the adjective; the noun, which is rather an absolute use of the adjective, tends to follow it in being without -s; moving eastwards; the eastward position; looking to the eastward(s). This usage prevails especially with the words made of a noun+-ward(s), but is also generally true of the older words in which the first part is adverbial, such as downward. Some words, however, have peculiarities; see AFTERWARDS, FOR- WARD, ONWARD, TOWARDS.
===
**-WARD(S).** Words ending with *-ward(s)* may most of them be used as adverbs, adjectives, or nouns. The -s is usually present in the adverb, & absent in the adjective; the noun, which is rather an absolute use of the adjective, tends to follow it in being without -s; *moving eastwards; the eastward position; looking to the eastward(s).* This usage prevails especially with the words made of a noun+*-ward(s)*, but is also generally true of the older words in which the first part is adverbial, such as *downward*. Some words, however, have peculiarities; see ^^AFTERWARDS, FORWARD, ONWARD, TOWARDS^^.
>>>

